[{"id":87624,"sender_id":458,"sender_full_name":"EgbertRijke","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/88dd841deda6a4843c4ba6506785145d?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Definitions.20of.20constructive.20fields/near/87624\">#1</a> The <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/field#constructive\">entry for constructive fields</a> contains a discussion about different options to define a field constructively, but the most obvious definitions is not among the options. One could simply say that a constructive field is a commutative ring <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span></span></span></span> such that</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⇔</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x\\neq 0)\\Leftrightarrow\\mathsf{isinvertible}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace nobreak\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇔</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathsf\">isinvertible</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>for all <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span></span></span></span>. Strangely, in several of the definitions on the nlab page it is proposed to replace this condition, but it is never said why this is necessary. </p>\n<p>On the other hand, the definition of a residue field is included. Here the condition is</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⇔</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x=0)\\Leftrightarrow\\neg\\mathsf{isinvertible}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇔</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">¬</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathsf\">isinvertible</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>for all <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span></span></span></span>. This condition implies that the predicate <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span> is double-negation stable, which seems to reduce the applicability of the definition more than the more natural condition which seems to be dismissed.</p>\n<p>So I was wondering why the condition <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⇔</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x\\neq 0)\\Leftrightarrow\\mathsf{isinvertible}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace nobreak\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇔</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathsf\">isinvertible</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is not considered as an option for a definition of constructive field. Perhaps there is something obviously wrong with it, that I’m missing.</p>","timestamp":1610117828,"subject":"Definitions of constructive fields","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]},{"id":87631,"sender_id":51,"sender_full_name":"Ulrik","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/e499ead5270df65e0b599d630ce0f40b?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Definitions.20of.20constructive.20fields/near/87631\">#2</a> Well, a main example we want is the real numbers, which form a Heyting field, whose apartness is not the denial inequality. The condition you write is like MRR87’s denial field, but without the requirement that the denial inequality be tight (so = not nec. stable), and without the requirement that (0) be a prime ideal.\nWhat’s a good example of such a field with non-stable equality?</p>","timestamp":1610125741,"subject":"Definitions of constructive fields","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]},{"id":96457,"sender_id":204,"sender_full_name":"IngoBlechschmidt","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/eb9f7e57a3c762c6d98139a2a349fae9?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Definitions.20of.20constructive.20fields/near/96457\">#3</a> One such example is the affine line, the canonical ring object in the big Zariski topos of a scheme. (Granted, technically, it’s not a ring but a sheaf of rings.)</p>","timestamp":1649801671,"subject":"Definitions of constructive fields","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]}]